{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01GkCbH2Jkw9RHc3WxDtoZhH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#include <math.h>\n2\t#include <stddef.h>\n3\t#include \"data.h\"\n4\t#include \"functions.h\"\n5\t#include \"collisions.h\"\n6\t\n7\t/**\n8\t * Helper function. Return the column number given an x coordinate. If the x coordinate lies outside the x dimension covered by the grid,\n9\t * then return a negative number: -2 for the left boundary and -1 for the right boundary.\n10\t */\n11\tint find_col(const double x, const int x_squares, const double square_length){\n12\t    double x_left_limit = -(x_squares*square_length/2); // The far leftest (if that word exists) of the grid\n13\t    double diff = x_left_limit -x;\n14\t    if(diff>0) return -2; // outside the left boundary\n15\t    int col_ind = floor(fabs(diff)/square_length);\n16\t    if(col_ind>=x_squares) return -1; // outisde the right boundary\n17\t    return col_ind;\n18\t}\n19\t\n20\t/**\n21\t * Helper function. Return the row number given an y coordinate. If the y coordinate lies below 0,\n22\t * then return a negative number: -2 for the left boundary and -1 for the right boundary.\n23\t */\n24\tint find_row(const double y, const int y_squares, const double square_length){\n25\t    int row_ind = floor(((double)y)/square_length);\n26\t    if(row_ind >= y_squares) return -1;\n27\t    if(row_ind < 0) return -2;\n28\t    return row_ind;\n29\t}\n30\t\n31\t/**\n32\t * Helper function. Given a point (x, y), find the index for the square that should contain it. If either coordinate lies outide the area covered\n33\t * by the grid, then return -1.\n34\t */\n35\tint find_square(const double x, const double y, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length){\n36\t    int row_ind = find_row(y,y_squares,square_length);\n37\t    int col_ind = find_col(x,x_squares,square_length);\n38\t    if(col_ind < 0 || row_ind < 0){\n39\t        return -1;\n40\t    }\n41\t    return row_ind * x_squares + col_ind;\n42\t}\n43\t\n44\t/**\n45\t * From the Grid, find all the pairs of particles that are colliding with each other, create a struct Contact for\n46\t * each one and save it into 'contacts'. Returns the number of collisions.\n47\t *\n48\t * The search for contacts is done as following:\n49\t *     1. Traverse each square in the grid. If it is empty (there is a null pointer in grid[square_idx]), then continue to the next square.\n50\t *          2. Iterate each particle inside the square. The particle iterated over will be 'p'.\n51\t *              3. If p is the only particle inside the square, continue to 5.\n52\t *                  4. Traverse all the square to find collisions with its  square-neighbors.\n53\t *              5. Traverse the particles that are inside squares that could be containing particles colliding with p. The squares can be found with the following method:\n54\t *                  a. Imagine a circle with twice the radius of p, called neighboring cicle. Every other coliding particle's center \n55\t *                     must be inside the neighboring circle, since all have the same radius. \n56\t *                  b. We have to find the set of squares such that part of its geometric area is overlaping with p's neighboring circle.\n57\t *                     We do not do this, but something similar. Instead of using a circle, we use the square in which the neighboring circle is inscribed.\n58\t *                  c. We find the rows and columns that contain the limits (upper, lower, left and right) of said square. These will define\n59\t *                     us the set of squares inside Grid that could contain p's colliding particles.\n60\t *                  d. Traverse all the particles inside each found square to find collisions.\n61\t */\n62\tsize_t compute_contacts(Particle const *const *const grid, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length, Contact* contacts){\n63\t    size_t k = 0; // current number of contacts\n64\t    // For each square\n65\t    for(int row=0; row<y_squares; row++){\n66\t        for(int col=0; col<x_squares; col++){\n67\t            size_t square_idx = row*x_squares+col;\n68\t            if(!grid[square_idx]) continue; // If the square is empty\n69\t            Particle* first = (Particle*)grid[square_idx]; // Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n70\t            Particle* p =(Particle*)grid[square_idx]; // Iterator. Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n71\t            while(p){\n72\t                Particle* other = first;\n73\t                while(other){ // First compare with particles within the same square\n74\t                    if(other != p){\n75\t                        const double overlap = compute_overlap(p, other);\n76\t                        if(overlap > 0){\n77\t                            contacts[k].p1_idx = p->idx;\n78\t                            contacts[k].p2_idx = other->idx;\n79\t                            contacts[k].overlap = overlap;\n80\t                            k++;\n81\t                        }\n82\t                    }\n83\t                    other = other->next;\n84\t                }\n85\t                // Then, compare with p's surrounding squares\n86\t                // Find all the squares which could contain particles colliding with p\n87\t                int left_col = find_col(p->x_coordinate - p->radius*2, x_squares, square_length);\n88\t                if(left_col == -2) left_col = 0;\n89\t                int right_col = find_col(p->x_coordinate + p->radius*2, x_squares, square_length);\n90\t                if(right_col == -1) right_col = x_squares-1;\n91\t                int bottom_row = find_row(p->y_coordinate - p->radius*2, y_squares, square_length);\n92\t                if(bottom_row==-2) bottom_row = 0;\n93\t                int top_row = find_row(p->y_coordinate + p->radius*2, y_squares, square_length);\n94\t                if(top_row==-1) top_row=y_squares-1;\n95\t                // Iterate over the squares\n96\t                for(int neighbor_row=bottom_row; neighbor_row<=top_row; neighbor_row++){\n97\t                    for(int neighbor_col=left_col; neighbor_col<=right_col;neighbor_col++){\n98\t                        size_t neighbor_square_idx = neighbor_row*x_squares+neighbor_col;\n99\t                        other = (Particle *)grid[neighbor_square_idx]; // Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n100\t                        if(other==NULL) continue; // If there are no particles inside this square\n101\t                        if(neighbor_square_idx==square_idx) continue; // If this is p's square, then this has already been traversed\n102\t                        while(other){\n103\t                            const double overlap = compute_overlap(p, other);\n104\t                            if(overlap > 0){\n105\t                                contacts[k].p1_idx = p->idx;\n106\t                                contacts[k].p2_idx = other->idx;\n107\t                                contacts[k].overlap = overlap;\n108\t                                k++;\n109\t                            }\n110\t                            other = other->next;\n111\t                        }\n112\t                    }\n113\t                }\n114\t                p = p->next;\n115\t            }\n116\t        }\n117\t    }\n118\t    return k;\n119\t}\n120\t\n121\t/**\n122\t * Fills the grid with particles. The side length of each square grid is twice the diameter of each particle,\n123\t * since all particles have the same radius. Running time is O(N), and memory space is O(N) but really 2*N because of 'lasts' pointers array.\n124\t */\n125\tvoid fill_grid(const size_t num_particles, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length,\n126\t        Particle const *const particles, Particle* *const grid, Particle* *const grid_lasts){\n127\t    // For each particle, find its grid's square. Keep in mind that grid is an array of pointers to the first element in the squares, and that\n128\t    // grid_lasts is an array of pointers to the last element in the squares.\n129\t    for(size_t i=0; i<num_particles; i++){\n130\t        Particle* p = (Particle*) &particles[i]; // Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n131\t        p->next=NULL; // Make sure we do not do anything funny\n132\t        int square_ind = find_square(p->x_coordinate, p->y_coordinate, x_squares, y_squares, square_length);\n133\t        if(square_ind< 0) continue; // The particle is outside the boundaries of our grid\n134\t        if(!grid_lasts[square_ind]){ // if is the first element in the square\n135\t            grid[square_ind] = p;\n136\t            grid_lasts[square_ind] = p;\n137\t        }else{\n138\t            grid_lasts[square_ind]->next=p;\n139\t            grid_lasts[square_ind] = p;\n140\t        }\n141\t    }\n142\t\n143\t}\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2d47bb6a-63f7-4f14-b847-cbb8d29c673e","uuid":"7206b8c3-3cce-4320-a8b9-62ec1d87a54b","timestamp":"2026-06-06T06:25:25.711Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-e98JkG/project/c_src/src/collisions.c","content":"#include <math.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\n#include \"data.h\"\n#include \"functions.h\"\n#include \"collisions.h\"\n\n/**\n * Helper function. Return the column number given an x coordinate. If the x coordinate lies outside the x dimension covered by the grid,\n * then return a negative number: -2 for the left boundary and -1 for the right boundary.\n */\nint find_col(const double x, const int x_squares, const double square_length){\n    double x_left_limit = -(x_squares*square_length/2); // The far leftest (if that word exists) of the grid\n    double diff = x_left_limit -x;\n    if(diff>0) return -2; // outside the left boundary\n    int col_ind = floor(fabs(diff)/square_length);\n    if(col_ind>=x_squares) return -1; // outisde the right boundary\n    return col_ind;\n}\n\n/**\n * Helper function. Return the row number given an y coordinate. If the y coordinate lies below 0,\n * then return a negative number: -2 for the left boundary and -1 for the right boundary.\n */\nint find_row(const double y, const int y_squares, const double square_length){\n    int row_ind = floor(((double)y)/square_length);\n    if(row_ind >= y_squares) return -1;\n    if(row_ind < 0) return -2;\n    return row_ind;\n}\n\n/**\n * Helper function. Given a point (x, y), find the index for the square that should contain it. If either coordinate lies outide the area covered\n * by the grid, then return -1.\n */\nint find_square(const double x, const double y, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length){\n    int row_ind = find_row(y,y_squares,square_length);\n    int col_ind = find_col(x,x_squares,square_length);\n    if(col_ind < 0 || row_ind < 0){\n        return -1;\n    }\n    return row_ind * x_squares + col_ind;\n}\n\n/**\n * From the Grid, find all the pairs of particles that are colliding with each other, create a struct Contact for\n * each one and save it into 'contacts'. Returns the number of collisions.\n *\n * The search for contacts is done as following:\n *     1. Traverse each square in the grid. If it is empty (there is a null pointer in grid[square_idx]), then continue to the next square.\n *          2. Iterate each particle inside the square. The particle iterated over will be 'p'.\n *              3. If p is the only particle inside the square, continue to 5.\n *                  4. Traverse all the square to find collisions with its  square-neighbors.\n *              5. Traverse the particles that are inside squares that could be containing particles colliding with p. The squares can be found with the following method:\n *                  a. Imagine a circle with twice the radius of p, called neighboring cicle. Every other coliding particle's center \n *                     must be inside the neighboring circle, since all have the same radius. \n *                  b. We have to find the set of squares such that part of its geometric area is overlaping with p's neighboring circle.\n *                     We do not do this, but something similar. Instead of using a circle, we use the square in which the neighboring circle is inscribed.\n *                  c. We find the rows and columns that contain the limits (upper, lower, left and right) of said square. These will define\n *                     us the set of squares inside Grid that could contain p's colliding particles.\n *                  d. Traverse all the particles inside each found square to find collisions.\n */\nsize_t compute_contacts(Particle const *const *const grid, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length, Contact* contacts){\n    size_t k = 0; // current number of contacts\n    // For each square\n    for(int row=0; row<y_squares; row++){\n        for(int col=0; col<x_squares; col++){\n            size_t square_idx = row*x_squares+col;\n            if(!grid[square_idx]) continue; // If the square is empty\n            Particle* first = (Particle*)grid[square_idx]; // Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n            Particle* p =(Particle*)grid[square_idx]; // Iterator. Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n            while(p){\n                Particle* other = first;\n                while(other){ // First compare with particles within the same square\n                    if(other != p){\n                        const double overlap = compute_overlap(p, other);\n                        if(overlap > 0){\n                            contacts[k].p1_idx = p->idx;\n                            contacts[k].p2_idx = other->idx;\n                            contacts[k].overlap = overlap;\n                            k++;\n                        }\n                    }\n                    other = other->next;\n                }\n                // Then, compare with p's surrounding squares\n                // Find all the squares which could contain particles colliding with p\n                int left_col = find_col(p->x_coordinate - p->radius*2, x_squares, square_length);\n                if(left_col == -2) left_col = 0;\n                int right_col = find_col(p->x_coordinate + p->radius*2, x_squares, square_length);\n                if(right_col == -1) right_col = x_squares-1;\n                int bottom_row = find_row(p->y_coordinate - p->radius*2, y_squares, square_length);\n                if(bottom_row==-2) bottom_row = 0;\n                int top_row = find_row(p->y_coordinate + p->radius*2, y_squares, square_length);\n                if(top_row==-1) top_row=y_squares-1;\n                // Iterate over the squares\n                for(int neighbor_row=bottom_row; neighbor_row<=top_row; neighbor_row++){\n                    for(int neighbor_col=left_col; neighbor_col<=right_col;neighbor_col++){\n                        size_t neighbor_square_idx = neighbor_row*x_squares+neighbor_col;\n                        other = (Particle *)grid[neighbor_square_idx]; // Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n                        if(other==NULL) continue; // If there are no particles inside this square\n                        if(neighbor_square_idx==square_idx) continue; // If this is p's square, then this has already been traversed\n                        while(other){\n                            const double overlap = compute_overlap(p, other);\n                            if(overlap > 0){\n                                contacts[k].p1_idx = p->idx;\n                                contacts[k].p2_idx = other->idx;\n                                contacts[k].overlap = overlap;\n                                k++;\n                            }\n                            other = other->next;\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n                p = p->next;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return k;\n}\n\n/**\n * Fills the grid with particles. The side length of each square grid is twice the diameter of each particle,\n * since all particles have the same radius. Running time is O(N), and memory space is O(N) but really 2*N because of 'lasts' pointers array.\n */\nvoid fill_grid(const size_t num_particles, const int x_squares, const int y_squares, const double square_length,\n        Particle const *const particles, Particle* *const grid, Particle* *const grid_lasts){\n    // For each particle, find its grid's square. Keep in mind that grid is an array of pointers to the first element in the squares, and that\n    // grid_lasts is an array of pointers to the last element in the squares.\n    for(size_t i=0; i<num_particles; i++){\n        Particle* p = (Particle*) &particles[i]; // Cast so the compiler does not yell because of referencing a const pointer\n        p->next=NULL; // Make sure we do not do anything funny\n        int square_ind = find_square(p->x_coordinate, p->y_coordinate, x_squares, y_squares, square_length);\n        if(square_ind< 0) continue; // The particle is outside the boundaries of our grid\n        if(!grid_lasts[square_ind]){ // if is the first element in the square\n            grid[square_ind] = p;\n            grid_lasts[square_ind] = p;\n        }else{\n            grid_lasts[square_ind]->next=p;\n            grid_lasts[square_ind] = p;\n        }\n    }\n\n}\n","numLines":144,"startLine":1,"totalLines":144}}}
